<!--
A page that edits itself. Run it through the one binary:

    beet --main=examples/ui/scene_editor.bsx                # the terminal, and the site
    beet --main=examples/ui/scene_editor.bsx --server=http  # the site alone

The same entry serves itself and boots in the tab: `<ServeBlobs>` publishes
this directory at `/repo`, the served page's `<Wasm/>` (`templates/EditorShell.bsx`)
resolves the entry through it and boots its `DomServer`, adopting the served
page node for node so the boot is invisible (`just test-scene-editor-browser`,
`just check-wasm-boot`).

The page is a scene: `<SceneBlob>` boots `scene_editor/scene.bsx` on the first
run, forks what it built into `scene_editor/scene.json` and boots from that
fork on every later run, so the fork is the living page and the `.bsx` stays
the authored original. Nothing here says what the page contains; it is a shell
around the scene document.

The scene carries its own editor: `<ToggleSceneEditor/>` is one entity among
the page's, kept in the scene document like any other, so removing your editor
is an ordinary edit. Open it and the tree on the left lists every entity the
document holds, by name else by key and kind (`#2 h1`, `#3 "Garden"`); select
one and the inspector on the right is a form over its components, each typed by
the schema registered under its key. Every control edits the document, never
the world: the world follows per component, in place, so typing into a text
node's `Value` repaints the heading mid-keystroke while the focus stays put.

So the demonstration is item 3's loop: select the heading's text, retype it,
add a `Name` through the component picker, reparent the paragraph under the
heading through its `ChildOf` picker, remove a component through its card, then
quit and run again: every edit came back from the fork.

`git diff examples/ui/scene_editor` is the record of a session and
`git checkout` is the reset; deleting `scene.json` re-forks the original.
-->
<CallOnReady {(HttpServer, TuiServer, DomServer, SweepDescendants)}>
	<!-- one entry, three launches: the site and the terminal run from the
	     native binary, which carries the tui host, and the browser from the web
	     runtime. Each launch names the one it has, so the entry keeps its shape
	     on all three and refuses a binary that could paint on none. -->
	<RequireCfg cfg="feature:tui || feature:web"/>
	<Router {Layout{template:"EditorShell"}}>
		<TemplateDir src="templates"/>
		<!-- the store the browser reads this entry through, and the wasm binary
		     its page boots (`assets` links to the workspace tree, `just build-wasm-ui`). -->
		<ServeBlobs prefix="repo"/>
		<AssetsDir src="assets" prefix="assets"/>
		<Route path="/" {FixedPage}>
			<SceneBlob path="scene_editor/scene.json" from="scene_editor/scene.bsx"/>
			<!-- what the keys do on each surface, one line per surface forked by
			     the `terminal-only`/`terminal-hidden` utilities: the terminal's
			     bindings are the page's own and the browser's are the browser's,
			     and the page says so because nothing else can. -->
			<footer class="text-body-small" bx:style="color=@token:OnSurfaceVariant">
				<span class="terminal-only">Tab moves · Enter activates · ↑↓ scrolls · Ctrl+C quits</span>
				<span class="terminal-hidden">Tab moves · Enter activates · ↑↓ scrolls · edits stay in this browser</span>
			</footer>
		</Route>
	</Router>
</CallOnReady>
